1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,440
Hey everyone.  We are going to be taking a look at price discrimination and how that affects societal welfare whenever monopoly engages in it. 

2
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,840
Let's go ahead and jump into it. 

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:16,328
We're going to start with a price and quantity axis and then we're going to add in our demand curve. 

4
00:00:16,504 --> 00:00:24,936
And for simplicity, we're going to make the marginal cost curve equal the average cost curve and we're going to make it horizontal. 

5
00:00:25,048 --> 00:00:27,220
This gives us a price of $10. 

6
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:31,930
Now we also have our marginal revenue curve which is downward sloping. 

7
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:36,566
And let's add in a price that the monopoly charges. 

8
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:38,726
Here we have $15. 

9
00:00:38,838 --> 00:00:41,238
Where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. 

10
00:00:41,294 --> 00:00:43,970
We have an output of 100. 

11
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:49,878
Now what we want to do is analyze the welfare impacts of what we see. 

12
00:00:50,014 --> 00:00:56,872
First we have our consumer surplus, the area below the demand curve and above the price being charged. 

13
00:00:57,016 --> 00:01:00,424
This gives us a total of $250. 

14
00:01:00,552 --> 00:01:02,680
That goes back to the consumers. 

15
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,544
Then we have our monopoly profit. 

16
00:01:05,672 --> 00:01:13,240
This is going to be whatever the monopoly charges minus its average cost times the quantity that it sells. 

17
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:16,088
And this gives us $500. 

18
00:01:16,264 --> 00:01:19,822
Now we do have a problem and that's this area here. 

19
00:01:19,976 --> 00:01:21,986
This is a dead weight loss. 

20
00:01:22,058 --> 00:01:24,722
This is welfare that no one picks up. 

21
00:01:24,826 --> 00:01:26,994
It's an efficiency problem. 

22
00:01:27,082 --> 00:01:31,010
That is we don't gain all the efficiency in this market. 

23
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:32,190
What's that about? 

24
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:34,706
How can we correct this? 

25
00:01:34,858 --> 00:01:42,546
Simply put, all we need to do is have the monopoly charge where demand equals marginal cost. 

26
00:01:42,698 --> 00:01:47,464
This gives us a quantity of 200 units of output. 

27
00:01:47,592 --> 00:01:49,240
Now notice what happens. 

28
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:53,380
All of these problems that we previously had go away. 

29
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:57,928
This becomes all economic profit for the, for the monopoly. 

30
00:01:58,024 --> 00:02:07,112
So we get one, no consumer surplus, two, all profit for the monopoly and three, a completely efficient outcome. 

31
00:02:07,256 --> 00:02:08,968
Because there's no dead weight loss. 

32
00:02:09,024 --> 00:02:11,342
It all goes back to the monopoly. 

33
00:02:11,456 --> 00:02:17,698
Now this may not be a good thing to have because consumers don't get to enjoy any surplus. 

34
00:02:17,874 --> 00:02:20,030
Well, that might be your problem. 

35
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:23,346
It's not what you like, it's the consumer. 

36
00:02:23,458 --> 00:02:28,190
But as far as efficiency goes, you can't do any better. 

37
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:34,226
Now what we do end up with is $1,000 going to the monopoly. 

38
00:02:34,338 --> 00:02:38,774
This is what we call first degree or perfect price discrimination. 

39
00:02:38,962 --> 00:02:50,366
This is an instance in when the monopoly can figure out each person willingness to pay for that item and they charge each person that price. 

40
00:02:50,518 --> 00:02:57,806
So the monopoly can go in and erase all the consumer surplus and turn it into monopoly profit. 

41
00:02:57,918 --> 00:03:01,650
Let's look at a few other types of price discrimination. 

42
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:05,392
Next we have second degree price price discrimination. 

43
00:03:05,456 --> 00:03:13,136
This is a quantity type price discrimination in which you get charged based on the amount that you consume. 

44
00:03:13,248 --> 00:03:24,400
If you look at your electricity bill and you look at your water bill, you have some base amount that you're going to be charged and then you get charged on the usage. 

45
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:37,460
If your water bill is really high one month, that's because you use a lot more water and your water company is charging you more for that extra amount of water that you use. 

46
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:44,212
If you go to a Costco or a Sam's Club, what you will see are more bulky items. 

47
00:03:44,276 --> 00:03:47,572
That is you can buy a whole lot more toilet paper or paper towels. 

48
00:03:47,716 --> 00:03:57,764
And you'll notice that the price per unit per roll is going to be less than if you went to a regular grocery store and purchased a four pack. 

49
00:03:57,892 --> 00:04:00,602
These are quantity discounts. 

50
00:04:00,796 --> 00:04:07,678
Then we also have our third degree or multi market price discrimination here. 

51
00:04:07,734 --> 00:04:15,006
The monopoly has to figure out that each consumer has some type of different price elasticity of demand. 

52
00:04:15,158 --> 00:04:19,598
And once it can determine that it charges those consumers different prices. 

53
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:25,118
Look at movie tickets, look at the times of the day and the prices associated with those times. 

54
00:04:25,254 --> 00:04:30,332
Look at the age groups associated with with the movie ticket prices. 

55
00:04:30,396 --> 00:04:38,108
And what we find out is that the matinees are cheaper or yeah, are cheaper, the evenings are more expensive. 

56
00:04:38,204 --> 00:04:44,492
And that's because there's a differing price elasticity of demand for those groups of consumers. 

57
00:04:44,636 --> 00:04:46,700
How about discounts, coupons? 

58
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:57,040
Yes, those are also a type of price discrimination because people who are going to use those have a different elasticity of demand than people who don't use those. 

59
00:04:57,160 --> 00:05:06,240
Therefore, what we can see is the monopoly goes in and they figure out the different price elasticities based off of who uses coupons. 

60
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:09,440
And then you have your early bird buffets. 

61
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:20,352
Here you have people going earlier out to eat and what they do is they get a cheaper meal than what you would get around these 6 and 7 o'clock hours. 

62
00:05:20,536 --> 00:05:23,744
We also have airfare here. 

63
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:30,288
We end up with different prices based on when you go and purchase your ticket. 

64
00:05:30,384 --> 00:05:37,936
Notice we have this is Southwest and we have April 5th as a time that we're going to purchase something. 

65
00:05:38,008 --> 00:05:42,992
And when this video was made, what we are looking at is a time close to April 5th. 

66
00:05:43,136 --> 00:05:50,202
And then we look at June 7th, which is the same trip, Atlanta to San Francisco. 

67
00:05:50,346 --> 00:05:54,602
But notice the want to get away airfare differences. 

68
00:05:54,746 --> 00:06:04,314
$492 for the first flight versus $200 for the other flight that leaves on a much later date. 

69
00:06:04,442 --> 00:06:12,362
So what we see are people who need it now, they get charged much more than the people who need it later. 

70
00:06:12,546 --> 00:06:16,176
Business people who are going to a meeting that just got called. 

71
00:06:16,328 --> 00:06:26,416
They have to pay that price because they have to go to that business meeting and what we see is third degree price discrimination taking place.  All right. 

